# gitleaks ignore list
#
# The secret scan is a BLOCKING gate. A finding that is not in this file reds
# the pull request, which is the point: a leaked credential must not be
# something a reviewer can scroll past.
#
# Each entry is a triaged false positive and MUST carry a comment explaining
# why the match is benign. If a finding cannot be justified, do not add it
# here — rotate the secret and remove it from the source.
#
# Format: <repo-relative-path>:<rule-id>:<start-line>
#
# Two things about that format, learned the hard way:
#
#   * It is an exact fingerprint. A path on its own matches nothing — gitleaks
#     compares the whole `path:rule:line` string — so there is no way to
#     exempt a file wholesale from this file.
#   * Because the line number is part of it, an edit that shifts a line in one
#     of these files invalidates its entry and the scan goes red on a finding
#     that was already triaged. Four of the six entries this list held before
#     the gate became blocking had rotted exactly that way and were silently
#     suppressing nothing. When that happens the fix is to correct the line
#     number, never to drop the entry or relax the job.

